WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.602
A monitoring baseline is a reference for expected assets, communication paths, protocols, roles, timing, and operational behavior.

00:00:08.602 --> 00:00:17.669
OT networks often have repeatable patterns, which makes meaningful changes visible when the baseline is accurate and maintained.

00:00:17.669 --> 00:00:20.691
Start with inventory and architecture.

00:00:20.691 --> 00:00:28.363
Identify expected devices, their function, normal peers, approved remote connections, engineering pathways, and trust boundaries.

00:00:28.363 --> 00:00:36.500
Observe several operating conditions, including startup, shutdown, maintenance, batch changes, failover, and seasonal or production variation.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:53.006
Anomalies may include a new device, a new communication pair, an unexpected protocol, activity outside an approved window, changed message frequency, unusual engineering access, or a command pattern that differs from the normal role.

00:00:53.006 --> 00:00:59.748
A change in behavior is evidence for investigation, not proof of malicious activity.

00:00:59.748 --> 00:01:06.025
Process faults, equipment replacement, troubleshooting, and authorized maintenance can all create anomalies.

00:01:06.025 --> 00:01:13.697
Operators and engineers can explain whether the observed activity matches the process and work plan.

00:01:13.697 --> 00:01:21.834
Monitoring teams should capture that context so the same approved pattern does not create repeated noise.

00:01:21.834 --> 00:01:29.506
Baselines can also preserve unsafe behavior if the learning period contains unmanaged access or misconfiguration.

00:01:29.506 --> 00:01:33.923
Compare observed patterns with approved architecture and policy.

00:01:33.923 --> 00:01:37.876
Known normal is not automatically known good.

00:01:37.876 --> 00:01:40.898
Update baselines through change control.

00:01:40.898 --> 00:01:46.710
Document new equipment, firmware, production paths, vendor access, and expected timing.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:53.917
Tune detection using evidence, but avoid suppressing entire categories simply because they are noisy.

00:01:53.917 --> 00:02:03.449
Effective anomaly detection balances sensitivity with operational understanding, producing a manageable set of changes that knowledgeable teams can investigate.
